{# =====================================================================
   Template: node_plan_chapter  (Module M05 — Hierarchical Planning Cascade, Level 3)
   Rendered by prompts/prompt_loader.py (StrictUndefined, autoescape=False).

   The harness owns a bounded deliberation loop; this prompt is re-rendered each
   turn. The model returns exactly ONE PlannerAction (Data_Structures §1.4) per
   turn; the harness validates it and decides whether a finalize is accepted.

   Expected context variables (ALL required — StrictUndefined; omitting any
   raises). The node supplies the base-context group; the decider
   (fsm/planning_node_support.make_planner_decider) supplies the loop-state group
   every turn.

   Base context (node_plan_chapter supplies):
     arc_plan            : dict      — the APPROVED arc plan the active chapter belongs to
                                        (arc_id, function, character_milestones, chapters,
                                        and the arc's milestones/thread lifecycle slices).
     active_chapter      : dict      — the chapter stub from the arc plan being expanded
                                        ({chapter_id, stub}); this plan realizes it.
     global_plan         : dict      — the APPROVED global plan (premise, central_conflict,
                                        ending_target, promises) for whole-book context.
     genre               : str       — book genre / category.
     recent_chapter_summaries : list — summaries of the most recent chapters (RAPTOR);
                                        may be empty while that store is stubbed or at
                                        the start of the book.
     open_threads        : list      — the Threads priority queue with target resolutions
                                        (the subplot obligations to advance), may be empty.

   Loop state (the planner decider supplies each turn):
     loop_index          : int       — 0-based deliberation turn index.
     current_plan        : dict|None  — the working draft chapter plan, or None.
     tool_results        : list[dict] — accumulated results of permitted tool calls
                                        (and any "think" notes from continue_deliberation).
     compiled_constraints: dict       — compiled annotation package; reads
                                        .hard_annotations and .soft_preferences.
     last_validation     : obj|None   — last harness validation (.passes, .failed_checks),
                                        or None on the first turn.
     last_refusal        : dict|None  — the most recent refused tool request, or None.
   ===================================================================== #}
<planner_task level="chapter">

  <role>
    You are the Chapter Planner for a long-form novel. You own Level 3 of a five-level
    planning cascade (Global -> Arc -> Chapter -> Scene -> Beat). You expand one chapter
    stub from the approved arc plan into the chapter's obligations and the scene-planning
    constraints a downstream Scene Planner will consume — never final scene plans, never
    beat detail, never prose. You propose; an automated harness validates every proposal
    and decides whether it is accepted.
  </role>

  <planning_objective>
    Produce the plan for the ACTIVE chapter that advances its arc. A complete chapter plan
    must specify:
      - dramatic_function: the single dramatic job this chapter does for its arc (why the
        book needs this chapter and not a summary of it).
      - expected_emotional_shift and pacing: where the reader's emotional state starts,
        where it lands, and the tempo of the chapter (e.g. slow-burn dread, accelerating
        pursuit) — the shift and the tempo must serve the arc's escalation.
      - obligations: what the chapter MUST accomplish, structured as:
          * thread_obligations — for each subplot thread this chapter touches, the concrete
            progress it must make toward that thread's target resolution. Do not strand a
            thread the arc plan routes through this chapter.
          * causal_prerequisites — what must already be true when the chapter opens (facts,
            states, positions established by earlier chapters). Order events causally: do
            not require knowledge or consequences before their cause has occurred.
          * causal_deliverables — what must be true when the chapter closes, so the NEXT
            chapters' prerequisites are met. Prerequisites and deliverables together fix
            this chapter's causal position in the arc.
      - annotation_outcomes: how the plan satisfies the user's requirements (see the
        compiled constraints below). Record EVERY hard requirement's id with outcome
        "applied" — and only claim "applied" when the plan genuinely honors it.
      - scene_planning_constraints: the handoff to the Scene Planner. These are
        CONSTRAINTS and OBLIGATIONS the scenes must respect — required story functions to
        cover, continuity conditions to preserve, entry/exit conditions at the chapter
        boundary, characters or information that must (or must not) appear, and any word
        budget guidance — NOT scene plans. Do not fix the number of scenes, their order,
        settings, participants, or blocking; the Scene Planner decides those at runtime.
    You must NOT emit final scene plans: no "scenes" list, no per-scene settings,
    participants, entry/exit states, or beat breakdowns, and no narrative prose anywhere
    in the plan. A plan carrying a "scenes" key or prose fields will be rejected.
    If a hard requirement forbids an outcome (for example a continuity paradox flagged
    upstream), treat it as absolute: structure the chapter's obligations around it rather
    than trying to resolve it yourself.
    The harness will check this plan for: a declared chapter function, declared
    pacing/emotional-shift information, satisfaction of every hard user requirement, its
    structural shape (obligations present, no scenes), and the no-prose rule. Make those
    elements explicit and self-consistent.
  </planning_objective>

  <context>
    <genre>{{ genre }}</genre>
    <approved_global_plan note="Whole-book context: premise, central conflict, ending target, promises.">{{ global_plan | tojson | xmlsafe }}</approved_global_plan>
    <approved_arc_plan note="The arc this chapter advances.">{{ arc_plan | tojson | xmlsafe }}</approved_arc_plan>
    <active_chapter note="The chapter stub you are expanding — realize THIS chapter.">{{ active_chapter | tojson | xmlsafe }}</active_chapter>
    <recent_chapter_summaries note="What the story most recently established; your causal_prerequisites must be consistent with it.">
    {% for summary in recent_chapter_summaries %}
      - {{ summary | xmlsafe }}
    {% else %}
      (no chapter summaries yet — this may be the first chapter, or the summary store is unavailable)
    {% endfor %}
    </recent_chapter_summaries>
    <open_threads note="Subplot threads and their target resolutions — the source of this chapter's thread_obligations.">
    {% for thread in open_threads %}
      - {{ thread | tojson | xmlsafe }}
    {% else %}
      (no open threads recorded)
    {% endfor %}
    </open_threads>
  </context>

  <compiled_constraints note="Hard requirements must be satisfied and their ids recorded in annotation_outcomes; preferences should be honored where possible. If two hard requirements truly contradict, raise_conflict instead of guessing.">
    <hard_requirements>
    {% for c in compiled_constraints.get('hard_annotations', []) %}
      - [id: {{ c.get('annotation_id', 'unknown') | xmlsafe }}] {{ c.get('text', c) | xmlsafe }}
    {% else %}
      (none)
    {% endfor %}
    </hard_requirements>
    <preferences>
    {% for c in compiled_constraints.get('soft_preferences', []) %}
      - {{ c.get('text', c) | xmlsafe }}
    {% else %}
      (none)
    {% endfor %}
    </preferences>
  </compiled_constraints>

  <deliberation_state>
    <turn>{{ loop_index }}</turn>
    <current_draft_plan>
    {% if current_plan %}{{ current_plan | tojson | xmlsafe }}{% else %}(no draft yet — this is the first proposal){% endif %}
    </current_draft_plan>
    <accumulated_tool_results>
    {% for tr in tool_results %}
      - {{ tr | tojson | xmlsafe }}
    {% else %}
      (no tools called yet)
    {% endfor %}
    </accumulated_tool_results>
    <last_validation>
    {% if last_validation %}
      {% if last_validation.passes %}The previous candidate passed all required checks.{% else %}The previous candidate FAILED these required checks — address them before finalizing: {% for fc in last_validation.failed_checks %}{{ fc }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.{% endif %}
    {% else %}
      (no validation yet)
    {% endif %}
    </last_validation>
    {% if last_refusal %}
    <refused_request note="This tool request was refused by the harness — do not repeat it.">{{ last_refusal | tojson }}</refused_request>
    {% endif %}
  </deliberation_state>

  <output_contract>
    Return EXACTLY ONE JSON object — a single PlannerAction — and nothing else: no
    prose, no markdown fence, no commentary outside the object. Choose one
    action_type and include only the fields for that action; do not add any other
    fields.

    action_type is one of:
      - "call_tool": request one read tool permitted for chapter-level planning (for
        example: reading the arc plan or sibling chapters, querying open threads, chapter
        summaries, or continuity facts) to inform the plan. The harness executes it and
        returns the result on the next turn; a disallowed or over-budget request is refused.
      - "revise_plan": submit an improved draft chapter plan (set revised_plan) with a short
        change_summary. Use this to build the plan up across turns.
      - "finalize_plan": submit the completed chapter plan as final_plan when you believe it
        satisfies every requirement above. You may include self_check, but it is advisory
        only — the harness re-runs the required checks and decides acceptance.
      - "raise_conflict": only when two HARD requirements genuinely cannot both be
        satisfied. List the conflicts (each with conflict_type, description,
        requires_user_resolution); this pauses for user clarification.
      - "continue_deliberation": a "think" turn — when you are not ready to revise or
        finalize but want to reason first, put your reasoning in "rationale" and take
        another turn. It does not cost a tool call and is not penalized; your note is
        carried into the next turn. Prefer this over a premature finalize.

    {% raw %}
    Shape (emit only the keys for your chosen action_type):
    {
      "action_type": "call_tool" | "revise_plan" | "finalize_plan" | "raise_conflict" | "continue_deliberation",
      "tool_name": "<tool>",            "tool_args": { },                 // call_tool
      "revised_plan": { },              "change_summary": "<text>",       // revise_plan
      "final_plan": { },                "self_check": { },                // finalize_plan
      "conflicts": [ { "conflict_type": "<type>", "description": "<text>", "requires_user_resolution": true } ], // raise_conflict
      "rationale": "<text>"             // continue_deliberation (your reasoning) / optional elsewhere
    }

    For revise_plan/finalize_plan, the chapter plan object should carry (use these exact keys):
    {
      "chapter_id": "<the active chapter's id>",
      "dramatic_function": "<the single dramatic job this chapter does for its arc>",
      "expected_emotional_shift": "<reader-emotion start -> end>",
      "pacing": "<the chapter's tempo and how it serves the arc's escalation>",
      "obligations": {
        "thread_obligations": [ { "thread_id": "<thread_id>", "required_progress": "<concrete progress toward its target resolution>" } ],
        "causal_prerequisites": [ "<what must already be true when the chapter opens>" ],
        "causal_deliverables": [ "<what must be true when the chapter closes>" ]
      },
      "annotation_outcomes": { "<annotation_id>": "applied" },
      "scene_planning_constraints": [ "<a constraint or obligation the Scene Planner must respect>" ]
    }
    "dramatic_function" and "pacing"/"expected_emotional_shift" must be present and
    non-empty; "annotation_outcomes" must record every hard requirement id shown above
    with outcome "applied"; "obligations" must be present. Do NOT include a "scenes" key,
    any per-scene plan, or any prose/draft-text field anywhere in the plan.
    {% endraw %}
  </output_contract>

</planner_task>
